{
  "dom_id": "step-q163069",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "The lamina $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,x-1\\le y\\le 1}$ has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.",
  "worked_solution": "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type I plane region and $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{x-1}^{1}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x}[{\\int }_{x-1}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=x-1}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2x}\\cdot [1-(x-1)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2x}(2x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}1-\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(2-1)-(1-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
